  {
   "name": "Seth McGowan",
   "position": "RB",
   "nfl_team": "IND",
   "proj": 5.65,
   "ros_rank": 142.0,
   "fp_waiver_rank": 14.0,
   "injury": "ACTIVE",
   "replaces": "Roschon Johnson",
   "ros_rank_gain": 104.0,
   "week_proj_gain": -3.37,
   "priority": "LOW",
   "availability": "ESPN_FREE_AGENT",
   "espn_pct_owned": 11.6,
   "fp_waiver_note": "The NFL trade deadline is Tuesday, and there's a good chance the Colts will move RB Jonathan Taylor before the deadline. Taylor seemed to be bidding adieu after the Colts' Week 9 loss to the Cowboys when he offered thanks to the city of Indianapolis while being interviewed at his locker.\n\nIf indeed Taylor is traded, seventh-round rookie Seth McGowan becomes the Colts' new lead back. McGowan has performed well in a backup role to this point, with 43-208-1 rushing for an average of 4.8 yards per carry. He scored his first touchdown of the season on Sunday after Taylor lost his shoe on a run to the Cowboys' 2-yard line. McGowan came in and scored on the next play.\n\nMcGowan has only 5-9-0 receiving, but he showed some pass-catching aptitude over three college seasons at Oklahoma, New Mexico State and Kentucky.\n\nThe 6-0, 223-pound McGowan has a nice combination of size and speed. He ran a 4.49 at this year's scouting combine and also had a 42 1/2-inch vertical jump.\n\nThe pessimistic view is that the Colts' offense could be a sinkhole if they trade Taylor. QB Daniel Jones and WR Josh Downs have been involved in trade rumors as well.\n\nBut lead RBs are usually hard to find nine weeks into the regular season, and McGowan instantly becomes a startable fantasy asset if the Colts trade away Taylor."
  },
  {
   "name": "Tyler Higbee",
   "position": "TE",
   "nfl_team": "LAR",
   "proj": 7.81,
   "ros_rank": 143.0,
   "fp_waiver_rank": 16.0,
   "injury": "ACTIVE",
   "replaces": "Greg Dulcich",
   "ros_rank_gain": 1.0,
   "week_proj_gain": -1.1,
   "priority": "LOW",
   "availability": "ESPN_FREE_AGENT",
   "espn_pct_owned": 9.8,
   "fp_waiver_note": "Tyler Higbee had 18-212-1 over a three-game stretch from Week 3 to Week 5. Since then, he's had 22 or fewer receiving yards in four consecutive games.\n\nDespite the downturn, this might be a good time to buy in on Higbee. WR Davante Adams is expected to be out for at least another 5-6 weeks with a severe hamstring injury. WR Puka Nacua sustained a knee injury of unknown severity on Sunday. TE Terrance Ferguson was on his way back from an ankle injury when he picked up a hamstring injury in practice last week.\n\nHead coach Sean McVay has leaned aggressively into multiple-TE sets the last two seasons and may have to do so out of necessity in the weeks to come.\n\nHigbee led the Rams' tight ends in snaps (60), routes (35) and targets (5) in Sunday's 20-13 win over the Commanders, although his five targets netted only a single 13-yard catch.\n\nStill, Higbee has a promising target outlook this week against an Arizona defense that has allowed the eighth-most fantasy points to tight ends."
  },
  {
   "name": "Terrance Ferguson",
   "position": "TE",
   "nfl_team": "LAR",
   "proj": 0.0,
   "ros_rank": 114.0,
   "fp_waiver_rank": 21.0,
   "injury": "OUT",
   "replaces": "Greg Dulcich",
   "ros_rank_gain": 30.0,
   "week_proj_gain": -8.91,
   "priority": "LOW",
   "availability": "ESPN_FREE_AGENT",
   "espn_pct_owned": 12.0,
   "fp_waiver_note": "In a tale from the \"I hate fantasy football\" files, Terrance Ferguson seemed set to return from a high-ankle sprain in Week 9 but injured his hamstring in practice and now seems likely to miss several more weeks. Fantasy managers with an available roster spot and TE woes might want to consider picking up Ferguson for a buck and stashing him. If he returns before WR Davante Adams is back from his hamstring injury sometime in December, Ferguson could eventually be a high-volume target earner. None of the Rams' other tight ends have been able to provide consistent fantasy value."
  },
  {
   "name": "Xavier Hutchinson",
   "position": "WR",
   "nfl_team": "HOU",
   "proj": 9.69,
   "ros_rank": 206.0,
   "fp_waiver_rank": 26.0,
   "injury": "ACTIVE",
   "replaces": "Ryan Flournoy",
   "ros_rank_gain": -113.0,
   "week_proj_gain": 1.19,
   "priority": "LOW",
   "availability": "ESPN_FREE_AGENT",
   "espn_pct_owned": 5.0,
   "fp_waiver_note": "The Texans are using an unwieldy five-man receiver rotation that includes Nico Collins, Xavier Hutchinson, Marvin Harrison Jr., Tank Dell and Kayshon Boutte.\n\nCollins is rostered everywhere, as he should be. Marvin Harrison Jr. is rostered in 64% of Yahoo leagues. Houston's other three receivers are rostered in less than half of Yahoo leagues. We don't think Dell or Boutte are worth picking up, but Hutchison is intriguing.\n\nHutchinson led Houston's receivers in snaps (57) and routes (34) Sunday vs. the Chargers. He had 3-29-1 on five targets. Hutchinson has scored a touchdown in two straight games. He's had three or more catches in all but one of his eight games this season.\n\nWhat's significant here is that Hutchinson is now serving as the Texans' primary slot receiver. Hutchinson also has favorable matchups the next two weeks. Entering Week 9, the Browns had allowed the 11th-most fantasy points per game to slot receivers, and the Colts had allowed the fifth-most.\n\nIt should be easy to sneak Hutchinson through with a $1 or $0 FAAB bid since he was overshadowed by a couple of his teammates in Week 9."
  },
